From 8fd29c958df18544630e3daee747b93a4056a753 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 3dpdf <3dpdf@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 13:18:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Add File > Import from STEP to the viewer, bridging
 OpenCASCADE into nanoPRC's write API

Lets the viewer open a STEP (ISO 10303) file directly: reads and
tessellates it with OpenCASCADE (OCCT), then writes the result out
through nanoPRC's own prc_api_write_prc_buffer + prc_api_pdf_embed_prc
(unmodified, pre-existing API -- this patch is pure integration, no
changes to nanoPRC's write path itself) as a real 3D PDF, which the
viewer then loads normally.

SCOPE: geometry only. Every solid in the file becomes its own PRC part
(so the Scene panel's part tree stays useful on a real assembly), but
STEP's own product/assembly names and hierarchy are not preserved
(parts are named "Solid N"), and neither are colors/materials/PMI --
nanoPRC's write API has no parameters for any of that to flow through
even if extracted. This is a geometry importer, not a STEP-to-3D-PDF
archival tool.

Implementation:
- demos/viewer/src/step_import.{h,cpp}: the real bridge, built only
  when OpenCASCADE is found. Reads via STEPControl_Reader, tessellates
  via BRepMesh_IncrementalMesh, then for each TopAbs_SOLID (falling
  back to the whole shape if the file has no explicit SOLID wrapper)
  flattens its faces' triangulations into one nanoPRC tessellation
  entry -- applying each face's own TopLoc_Location (STEP assemblies
  place shared/instanced shapes via a transform on the reference, not
  by duplicating geometry -- skipping this would stack every instance
  at the same spot) and fixing triangle winding for TopAbs_REVERSED
  faces (needed for correct flat-shaded normals under nanoPRC's
  recalculated-normals path; half the model's faces point inward
  without this).
- demos/viewer/src/step_import_stub.cpp: built instead when
  OpenCASCADE isn't found, so the menu item and its call sites in
  main.cpp are unconditional -- absence only surfaces as a clear
  runtime message when actually invoked, not a vanished menu item or a
  failed build.
- CMakeLists.txt / demos/viewer/CMakeLists.txt: find_package(OpenCASCADE
  QUIET CONFIG) at top-level scope (same reasoning as the existing SDL3
  find_package -- demos/viewer needs the imported targets, and it's a
  sibling of thirdparty/, not a child), best-effort like every other
  optional dependency in this project; link only the specific OCCT
  modules actually used (STEP read, tessellation, basic topology), not
  the full module list find_package(OpenCASCADE) exposes.
- main.cpp: a plain ImGui main menu bar (File > Import from STEP...),
  a zenity file picker and error dialog run via fork+execvp (never a
  shell -- no injection concern regardless of what ends up in a picked
  path or error string), and process relaunch via execv(/proc/self/exe,
  ...) with the converted PDF as the new argument on success.
  Deliberately NOT a hot-reload of the running Scene: Scene::load() was
  written as a one-shot startup path that calls exit(1) on any internal
  failure (see scene.cpp), so making it safely re-enterable would mean
  auditing and rewriting every one of those failure paths -- relaunching
  reuses that exact, already-proven startup path instead of adding a
  second, less-tested one. The menu bar's own hover state is folded into
  the existing mouse_over_debug gate (same mechanism debugMenu() already
  uses) so opening File doesn't also start an arcball drag on the model
  underneath it.

Verified end-to-end, repeatedly, against a real file (Onshape STEP
export, 12 solids, 897 faces): a standalone bridge prototype first (STEP
parse 0.9s, tessellate 0.2s, nanoPRC write ~0.1s), confirmed correct via
scan_prc + a rendered screenshot; then the per-solid-split version
(scan_prc: parts=24 tess=12, one tessellation per solid) via the same
checks; then the full in-viewer flow interactively under Xvfb --
launched the viewer, opened File > Import from STEP, picked the real
file through the actual zenity dialog, watched it convert and the
process relaunch itself (confirmed via the new command line:
nano_prc_viewer /tmp/3dpdf-viewer-import-*.pdf), and confirmed the
model rendered correctly.

A real bug was found and fixed during this work, in the integration
code, not nanoPRC: prc_api_write_tessellation.num_positions is a VERTEX
count (nanoPRC's own prc_encode_preprocess_ex multiplies it by 3
internally), but its doc comment only says "3 doubles per vertex"
without stating that unit for num_positions itself -- passing the
doubles count instead segfaulted the compressed-tessellation encoder
(an out-of-bounds read in its own bbox-computation loop) on a real
36k-vertex mesh. Not a nanoPRC bug, but worth another engineer's eyes
if they hit the same misreading.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
